CHUNK({"chunk":"A","module":"m1","topics":[{"id":"m1-evs-intro","title":"Environmental studies: definition, scope and importance","short":"What EVS is","blurb":"Environmental science vs environmental studies vs environmental engineering, the ten areas EVS covers, why it matters, and why it needs many subjects at once.","source":"Lecture W1S1, pages 1–5","tabs":[{"title":"Three definitions","html":"\n          <p class=\"lead\"><b>Science</b> studies it, <b>studies</b> adds society and policy, <b>engineering</b> builds the fix.</p>\n          <table class=\"tbl\"><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Key words</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n            <tr><td><b>Environmental science</b></td><td>Humans + natural systems; understand and solve problems.</td></tr>\n            <tr><td><b>Environmental studies</b></td><td>Interdisciplinary; <b>ethical, social, political, economic</b> sides; social + natural sciences, law, policy.</td></tr>\n            <tr><td><b>Environmental engineering</b></td><td><b>Preserve and enhance</b> quality: waste, pollution prevention, water/air purification.</td></tr>\n          </tbody></table>\n          <h3>Who said what</h3>\n          <ul>\n            <li><b>Encyclopedia Britannica</b>: interdisciplinary field drawing on ecology, geology, meteorology, biology, chemistry, engineering, physics.</li>\n            <li><b>Spellman &amp; Stoudt</b>: study of <b>how the Earth works</b> and how we affect its life support systems.</li>\n          </ul>\n          <div class=\"note trap\"><p>Studies = people, ethics, policy. Science = how nature works. Engineering = technology.</p></div>\n        "},{"title":"Scope","html":"\n          <p class=\"lead\">The scope of EVS is ten areas. Each pairs a job with the subject that does it.</p>\n          <table class=\"tbl\"><thead><tr><th>What is studied</th><th>Area</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n            <tr><td>Relationships between living things and their surroundings</td><td><b>Ecology and ecosystem studies</b></td></tr>\n            <tr><td>Weather patterns, air pollution, climate change</td><td><b>Atmospheric science</b></td></tr>\n            <tr><td>Geologic processes, soil, landforms, natural resources</td><td><b>Geoscience</b></td></tr>\n            <tr><td>Managing freshwater, water quality, conserving water</td><td><b>Hydrology and water resources</b></td></tr>\n            <tr><td>Sources and impacts of air, water and soil pollution, and solutions</td><td><b>Pollution control</b></td></tr>\n            <tr><td>Maintaining species and their natural environments</td><td><b>Biodiversity and conservation</b></td></tr>\n            <tr><td>Chemical processes in the environment and their impacts</td><td><b>Environmental chemistry</b></td></tr>\n            <tr><td>Legislation, rules and moral dilemmas of environmental protection</td><td><b>Environmental policy and management</b></td></tr>\n            <tr><td>Meeting current demands without endangering future generations</td><td><b>Sustainable development</b></td></tr>\n            <tr><td>Environmental effects of population growth and urban development</td><td><b>Human population and urbanization</b></td></tr>\n          </tbody></table>\n        "},{"title":"Importance","html":"\n          <p class=\"lead\">Seven reasons EVS matters.</p>\n          <ol>\n            <li>Understand global issues: <b>pollution, deforestation, climate change, biodiversity loss</b>.</li>\n            <li><b>Sustainable use</b> of resources (today's needs, without harming future generations).</li>\n            <li>Conserve <b>soil, water, air and energy</b>.</li>\n            <li><b>Identify pollution sources</b>; build tech and policy to cut them.</li>\n            <li>Protect <b>human health</b> from pollutants.</li>\n            <li>Encourage <b>responsible behaviour</b>.</li>\n            <li><b>Predict and manage natural disasters</b> (floods, droughts, earthquakes).</li>\n          </ol>\n        "},{"title":"Multidisciplinary approach","html":"\n          <p class=\"lead\"><b>Multidisciplinary approach</b> = integrating knowledge and methods from many disciplines, because issues are <b>complex and interconnected</b>.</p>\n          <p>EVS deals with <b>every issue that affects an organism</b>. It is an <b>applied science</b>: it seeks practical answers for living sustainably on Earth's <b>finite resources</b>.</p>\n          <h3>Disciplines involved</h3>\n          <table class=\"tbl\"><thead><tr><th>Discipline</th><th>Contributes</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n            <tr><td><b>Biology</b></td><td>Ecosystems, biodiversity, species interactions</td></tr>\n            <tr><td><b>Chemistry</b></td><td>Pollution, chemical cycles (C, N), toxicity</td></tr>\n            <tr><td><b>Physics</b></td><td>Energy flow, climate modelling, solar radiation</td></tr>\n            <tr><td><b>Geology</b></td><td>Soil, minerals, natural disasters, landforms</td></tr>\n            <tr><td><b>Geography</b></td><td>Mapping, spatial relationships, planning</td></tr>\n            <tr><td><b>Economics</b></td><td>Cost-benefit analysis, resource management</td></tr>\n            <tr><td><b>Political science</b></td><td>Laws, international treaties, policy</td></tr>\n            <tr><td><b>Sociology</b></td><td>Human behaviour, culture, public awareness</td></tr>\n            <tr><td><b>Engineering</b></td><td>Pollution control tech, renewable energy</td></tr>\n            <tr><td><b>Ethics / philosophy</b></td><td>Moral responsibility, environmental justice</td></tr>\n          </tbody></table>\n        "},{"title":"Why many subjects","html":"\n          <p class=\"lead\">Six reasons the multidisciplinary approach is needed.</p>\n          <ol>\n            <li><b>Holistic understanding</b>: climate change hits agriculture, health, water at once.</li>\n            <li><b>Effective problem solving</b>: water scarcity = <b>hydrology</b> (science) + <b>policy</b> (law) + <b>infrastructure</b> (engineering).</li>\n            <li><b>Sustainable development</b>: ecology + economy + social equity.</li>\n            <li><b>Informed decision-making</b> by governments, industries, individuals.</li>\n            <li><b>Policy formulation and implementation</b>: science + society + law + economics.</li>\n            <li><b>Adaptability to emerging challenges</b>: <b>microplastics</b>, pandemics linked to habitat loss.</li>\n          </ol>\n        "}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Field that stresses the ethical, social, political and economic sides of environmental problems?","back":"<b>Environmental studies</b>."},{"front":"Spellman &amp; Stoudt define environmental science as…","back":"The interdisciplinary study of <b>how the Earth works</b> and how we are affecting the Earth's life support systems."},{"front":"Field that uses engineering and scientific principles to preserve and enhance environmental quality?","back":"<b>Environmental engineering</b>."}],"cardCount":10,"formulaCount":0},{"id":"m1-env-issues","title":"Major environmental issues","short":"Environmental issues","blurb":"Eight issues with their causes, effects and Indian examples, the global picture with its numbers, India's situation, and loss of biodiversity.","source":"Lecture W1S1, pages 5–11","tabs":[{"title":"Climate change","html":"\n          <p class=\"lead\">Each issue: <b>cause → effects → example</b>.</p>\n          <h3>1. Climate change</h3>\n          <p><b>Cause.</b> <b>Greenhouse gases</b> (CO<sub>2</sub>, CH<sub>4</sub>, N<sub>2</sub>O) from <b>fossil fuel burning, deforestation, industrial processes</b>; they trap heat.</p>\n          <p><b>Effects.</b></p>\n          <ul>\n            <li><b>Increasing global temperatures</b> (global warming).</li>\n            <li><b>Melting ice caps, rising sea levels</b>.</li>\n            <li><b>Severe weather</b>: droughts, floods, hurricanes.</li>\n            <li><b>Influence on biodiversity and farming</b>.</li>\n          </ul>\n          <div class=\"note example\"><p>IPCC: average global temperature up approximately <b>1.1 °C</b> since pre-industrial times.</p></div>\n        "},{"title":"Deforestation","html":"\n          <h3>2. Deforestation</h3>\n          <p><b>Cause.</b> <b>Logging, agriculture, urban expansion, mining</b>.</p>\n          <p><b>Effects.</b></p>\n          <ul>\n            <li><b>Loss of biodiversity and habitats</b>.</li>\n            <li><b>Disruption of the water cycle</b>: less moisture, changed rainfall.</li>\n            <li><b>Increased CO<sub>2</sub></b> from <b>loss of carbon sinks</b>.</li>\n            <li><b>Soil erosion and desertification</b>.</li>\n          </ul>\n          <div class=\"note example\"><p><b>Amazon rainforest</b> deforestation contributes significantly to global carbon emissions.</p></div>\n        "},{"title":"Waste, air and water","html":"\n          <h3>3. Waste management</h3>\n          <p><b>Types:</b> municipal solid, plastic, organic waste, landfill overflow.</p>\n          <p><b>Issues.</b></p>\n          <ul>\n            <li><b>Landfills overflowing</b>.</li>\n            <li><b>Methane</b> from decomposing organic matter (greenhouse gas).</li>\n            <li><b>Inadequate segregation and recycling</b>.</li>\n          </ul>\n          <p><b>Remedies.</b> <b>Reduce-Reuse-Recycle (3Rs)</b>; <b>composting and waste-to-energy</b>.</p>\n          <div class=\"note example\"><p><b>CPCB (2021)</b>: India produces over <b>150,000 tonnes</b> of municipal solid waste per day.</p></div>\n          <h3>4. Industrial and vehicular pollution</h3>\n          <p><b>Causes.</b> Industries: <b>emissions, effluents, particulate matter (PM2.5, PM10)</b>. Vehicles: <b>CO, NOx, hydrocarbons</b>.</p>\n          <p><b>Effects.</b> <b>Respiratory diseases, smog, acid rain</b>; contaminate <b>air, water, soil</b>.</p>\n          <div class=\"note example\"><p><b>Delhi</b> often among the world's most polluted cities (vehicles, industry).</p></div>\n          <h3>5. Water pollution</h3>\n          <p><b>Causes.</b></p>\n          <ul>\n            <li><b>Agricultural runoff</b>: pesticides, fertilizers (<b>nitrates, phosphates</b>).</li>\n            <li><b>Industrial effluents and sewage</b> dumped in water.</li>\n          </ul>\n          <p><b>Effects.</b></p>\n          <ul>\n            <li><b>Eutrophication</b> (algal growth).</li>\n            <li><b>Contaminated drinking water</b>.</li>\n            <li><b>Health</b>: cancer, reproductive issues, endocrine disruption.</li>\n          </ul>\n          <div class=\"note example\"><p><b>Punjab's groundwater</b>: pesticide residues like <b>Endosulfan and DDT</b>.</p></div>\n        "},{"title":"Hazardous, crackers, e-waste","html":"\n          <h3>6. Hazardous waste</h3>\n          <p><b>a. Tannery sludge dumping.</b> Improper disposal of <b>chromium</b>-laden sludge from leather tanneries → soil and groundwater contaminated with <b>heavy metals</b>; harms agriculture and health.</p>\n          <div class=\"note example\"><p><b>Ranipet (Tamil Nadu)</b>: severe chromium pollution in groundwater from old tannery dumps.</p></div>\n          <p><b>b. Biomedical waste.</b> From <b>hospitals, clinics, laboratories</b>. Hazards: <b>infections (HIV, hepatitis)</b>, toxic chemicals, radioactive material. Managed by <b>segregation, incineration, autoclaving</b>.</p>\n          <div class=\"note example\"><p><b>COVID-19</b>: surge in biomedical waste, straining disposal.</p></div>\n          <h3>7. Cracking down on crackers</h3>\n          <p><b>Issue.</b> Smoke, particulate matter, toxic gases (<b>SO<sub>2</sub>, NO, Pb</b>).</p>\n          <ul>\n            <li><b>Air pollution spikes</b> during festivals (Diwali).</li>\n            <li><b>Respiratory distress</b> in vulnerable people.</li>\n            <li><b>Noise pollution</b>: animals, elderly.</li>\n          </ul>\n          <p><b>Response.</b> <b>Supreme Court</b> bans/limits; <b>green crackers</b> promoted.</p>\n          <div class=\"note example\"><p>Delhi AQI reaches <b>\"Severe\"</b> after Diwali every year.</p></div>\n          <h3>8. E-waste</h3>\n          <p><b>Sources.</b> Discarded mobiles, computers, TVs.</p>\n          <ul>\n            <li><b>Toxic metals</b> (lead, mercury, cadmium) leach into soil and water.</li>\n            <li><b>Informal recycling</b>: health hazards to workers.</li>\n          </ul>\n          <p><b>Solutions.</b> <b>Formal recycling centres</b>, <b>extended producer responsibility (EPR)</b>: producer responsible for discarded products.</p>\n          <div class=\"note example\"><p>India: <b>third-largest</b> e-waste generator, over <b>1 million tonnes/year</b> (CPCB, 2020).</p></div>\n        "},{"title":"Global scenario","html":"\n          <table class=\"tbl\"><thead><tr><th>Problem</th><th>Key fact</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n            <tr><td>Climate change</td><td>Fossil fuel GHGs; glacier melt, sea-level rise, extreme weather. <b>UNFCCC</b>, <b>Paris Agreement (2015)</b>: limit warming <b>below 2 °C</b>; IPCC urges action.</td></tr>\n            <tr><td>Biodiversity loss</td><td><b>1 million species</b> at risk (deforestation, pollution, overfishing, habitat destruction)</td></tr>\n            <tr><td>Plastic and marine pollution</td><td>Over <b>8 million tons</b> plastic into oceans/year; microplastics in marine life and <b>human blood</b></td></tr>\n            <tr><td>Air pollution</td><td><b>99%</b> of people breathe unsafe air; smog in Beijing, Cairo</td></tr>\n            <tr><td>E-waste and resource depletion</td><td><b>53 million metric tons</b> e-waste in 2020; mineral overextraction, geopolitical tension</td></tr>\n          </tbody></table>\n        "},{"title":"India and biodiversity loss","html":"\n          <p class=\"lead\">Causes: <b>population density, industrial growth, urban expansion</b>.</p>\n          <table class=\"tbl\"><thead><tr><th>Problem</th><th>India's numbers</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n            <tr><td>Air pollution</td><td><b>14 of the world's 20</b> most polluted cities; Delhi (vehicles, <b>crop burning</b>, industry)</td></tr>\n            <tr><td>Water pollution</td><td><b>70%</b> of surface water polluted, esp. <b>Ganga and Yamuna</b>; pesticides in groundwater</td></tr>\n            <tr><td>Deforestation and biodiversity</td><td>Mining, farming, infrastructure; <b>10%</b> of flora and fauna at risk</td></tr>\n            <tr><td>Waste management</td><td>Over <b>160,000 tonnes</b>/day, ~<b>60%</b> collected, under <b>30%</b> processed; e-waste over 1 million t/year</td></tr>\n            <tr><td>Climate vulnerability</td><td><b>7th</b> in climate risk: floods, droughts, cyclones</td></tr>\n            <tr><td>Hazardous waste</td><td>Medical, industrial waste, e.g. chromium in <b>Ranipet</b></td></tr>\n          </tbody></table>\n          <div class=\"note heads-up\"><p>Daily solid waste: over 150,000 t (CPCB 2021, waste issue) vs over 160,000 t (India scenario). Quote each in context.</p></div>\n          <h3>Loss of biodiversity</h3>\n          <p>Decline or disappearance of plant, animal and microorganism species. Causes: <b>habitat destruction, pollution, climate change, overexploitation, invasive species</b>. Weakens ecosystems, disturbs food chains.</p>\n          <p>Problems are interlinked → need a <b>multidisciplinary approach</b> for integrated, sustainable solutions.</p>\n        "}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Three greenhouse gases named as the cause of climate change?","back":"<b>CO<sub>2</sub>, CH<sub>4</sub>, N<sub>2</sub>O</b>."},{"front":"Global temperature rise since pre-industrial times (IPCC)?","back":"About <b>1.1 °C</b>."},{"front":"Example for deforestation?","back":"<b>Amazon rainforest</b>, a big contributor to global carbon emissions."}],"cardCount":12,"formulaCount":0},{"id":"m1-earth-spheres","title":"Earth as a life support system: atmosphere and hydrosphere","short":"Atmosphere & water","blurb":"The four spheres, what air is made of, the five layers of the atmosphere with their heights and temperatures, and the hydrosphere and water cycle.","source":"Lecture W1S3, pages 1–8","tabs":[{"title":"Four spheres","html":"\n          <p class=\"lead\">Earth supplies four needs, each from a <b>sphere</b> (\"sphere\" = <b>to surround or encompass</b>).</p>\n          <table class=\"tbl\"><thead><tr><th>Need</th><th>Sphere</th><th>Root word</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n            <tr><td>Air to breathe</td><td><b>Atmosphere</b></td><td>atmo → steam and vapour</td></tr>\n            <tr><td>Water to drink</td><td><b>Hydrosphere</b></td><td>hydro → water</td></tr>\n            <tr><td>Food sources</td><td><b>Biosphere</b></td><td>bio → life</td></tr>\n            <tr><td>Solid ground and nutrients</td><td><b>Lithosphere</b></td><td>litho → rocks and minerals</td></tr>\n          </tbody></table>\n          <p>The <b>biosphere</b> holds all living things and <b>depends on the other three</b>.</p>\n          <figure data-fig=\"m1-four-spheres\"></figure>\n        "},{"title":"Air and its layers","html":"\n          <p class=\"lead\">Atmosphere = <b>gaseous layer</b> around Earth: gases + tiny water particles, a thin protective envelope.</p>\n          <p>Provides oxygen, <b>shields from harmful solar radiation</b>, regulates temperature, drives weather and water cycle.</p>\n          <div class=\"kv\">\n            <div><span>Nitrogen</span><b>78%</b> by volume</div>\n            <div><span>Oxygen</span><b>21%</b></div>\n            <div><span>Others</span><b>1%</b>: water vapour, CO<sub>2</sub>, methane (matter for climate, energy balance)</div>\n          </div>\n          <figure data-fig=\"m1-air-composition\"></figure>\n          <p>Bottom to top: <b>troposphere → stratosphere → mesosphere → thermosphere → exosphere</b>.</p>\n          <figure data-fig=\"m1-atmosphere-layers-photo\"></figure>\n          <div class=\"mnemonic\"><p>\"<b>T</b>he <b>S</b>trong <b>M</b>an <b>T</b>hrows <b>E</b>ggs\": Tropo, Strato, Meso, Thermo, Exo.</p></div>\n        "},{"title":"Troposphere, stratosphere","html":"\n          <div class=\"vs\">\n            <div class=\"side\"><h4>Troposphere (0–15 km)</h4>\n              <ul>\n                <li><b>Lowest</b>; all life, birds, airplanes.</li>\n                <li><b>Most of the atmosphere's mass</b>, almost all weather (rain, snow, storms, clouds, wind).</li>\n                <li>Warmest near ground; temperature <b>falls with height</b>.</li>\n                <li>Height varies with latitude and weather.</li>\n                <li>Top: <b>tropopause</b>, a lid keeping weather out of the stratosphere.</li>\n              </ul></div>\n            <div class=\"side\"><h4>Stratosphere (15–50 km)</h4>\n              <ul>\n                <li>Holds the <b>ozone layer</b>.</li>\n                <li>Ozone absorbs most harmful <b>UV radiation</b>.</li>\n                <li>Temperature <b>rises with height</b> (ozone absorbs solar energy).</li>\n                <li><b>Stable and dry</b>: high-altitude aircraft, <b>weather balloons</b>.</li>\n                <li>Top: <b>stratopause</b>.</li>\n              </ul></div>\n          </div>\n          <figure data-fig=\"m1-stratosphere-profile\"></figure>\n          <figure data-fig=\"m1-ozone-uv\"></figure>\n          <div class=\"note why\"><p>Troposphere is heated by the ground (cooler upward); stratosphere by ozone absorbing UV (warmer upward).</p></div>\n        "},{"title":"Mesosphere and above","html":"\n          <div class=\"vs\">\n            <div class=\"side\"><h4>Mesosphere (50–85 km)</h4>\n              <ul>\n                <li><b>Coldest</b>, about <b>−90 °C</b>.</li>\n                <li>Ice clouds: <b>noctilucent clouds</b>, seen at twilight, among the highest clouds.</li>\n                <li>Very low pressure, thin air, hard to study.</li>\n                <li><b>Meteors burn up</b> (shooting stars).</li>\n                <li>Top: <b>mesopause</b>.</li>\n              </ul></div>\n            <div class=\"side\"><h4>Thermosphere</h4>\n              <ul>\n                <li>Temperature <b>rises with height</b> (absorbs <b>UV and X-ray</b>).</li>\n                <li>Upper part <b>500 °C to 2,000 °C</b> or higher.</li>\n                <li><b>Auroras</b> (Northern and Southern Lights).</li>\n                <li>Satellites, spacecraft, <b>International Space Station</b> orbit here.</li>\n              </ul></div>\n            <div class=\"side\"><h4>Exosphere</h4>\n              <ul>\n                <li><b>Final frontier</b>, outermost.</li>\n                <li>Air gradually <b>leaks into space</b>; no clear upper boundary.</li>\n                <li>Extremely thin; satellites orbit here too.</li>\n              </ul></div>\n          </div>\n          <figure data-fig=\"m1-mesosphere-diagram\"></figure>\n        "},{"title":"Layers at a glance","html":"\n          <table class=\"tbl\"><thead><tr><th>Layer</th><th>Altitude (km)</th><th>Temperature (°C)</th><th>Feature</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n            <tr><td>Troposphere</td><td><b>0–15</b></td><td>15 to −56</td><td>Weather occurs here</td></tr>\n            <tr><td>Stratosphere</td><td><b>15–50</b></td><td>−56 to −2</td><td>Ozone layer</td></tr>\n            <tr><td>Mesosphere</td><td><b>50–85</b></td><td>−2 to −92</td><td>Meteors burn</td></tr>\n            <tr><td>Thermosphere</td><td><b>85–800</b></td><td>−92 to 1200</td><td>Auroras</td></tr>\n          </tbody></table>\n          <figure data-fig=\"m1-atmosphere-poster\"></figure>\n          <div class=\"note heads-up\"><p>Numbers disagree: text says upper thermosphere 500–2,000 °C, table 1200 °C; mesosphere \"about −90 °C\" vs −92 °C; poster shows troposphere 0–12 km, stratosphere 12–50 km. Use: troposphere <b>0–15 km</b>, stratosphere <b>15–50 km</b>, mesosphere <b>50–85 km</b>.</p></div>\n        "},{"title":"Hydrosphere and water cycle","html":"\n          <p class=\"lead\">Hydrosphere = <b>all the water</b> on the surface, underground and in the air: liquid, ice, vapour.</p>\n          <div class=\"kv\">\n            <div><span>Oceans</span><b>~97%</b></div>\n            <div><span>Lakes and rivers</span>~2%</div>\n            <div><span>Freshwater usable by humans</span>only <b>1%</b> (rivers, lakes, streams, underground)</div>\n          </div>\n          <p>Sustains life, <b>distributes heat</b> (regulates climate); includes saltwater, freshwater, glaciers, ice caps, groundwater, water in lower atmosphere.</p>\n          <div class=\"note heads-up\"><p>Notes say ~2% in lakes and rivers; standard figures put most of that in glaciers and ice caps. Use the notes' numbers.</p></div>\n          <h3>Water (hydrologic) cycle</h3>\n          <ol class=\"steps\">\n            <li><b>Evaporation</b> Sun's heat turns water into vapour.</li>\n            <li><b>Condensation</b> Vapour cools into droplets: clouds.</li>\n            <li><b>Precipitation</b> Rain, snow, etc.</li>\n            <li><b>Runoff / infiltration</b> Into rivers and streams or into the ground.</li>\n            <li><b>Collection</b> Gathers in oceans or lakes; cycle repeats.</li>\n          </ol>\n          <figure data-fig=\"m1-water-cycle-spheres\"></figure>\n        "}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What does \"sphere\" mean?","back":"To <b>surround or encompass</b>."},{"front":"Root meaning of \"atmo\"?","back":"<b>Steam and vapour</b>."},{"front":"Which sphere depends on the other three?","back":"The <b>biosphere</b>."}],"cardCount":12,"formulaCount":0},{"id":"m1-geosphere","title":"Geosphere","short":"Geosphere","blurb":"Earth's interior layers, rocks, minerals, soil and landforms, the processes that shape the surface, why the geosphere matters, and how it interacts with the other spheres.","source":"Lecture W1S3, pages 8–12","tabs":[{"title":"What it is and its layers","html":"\n          <p class=\"lead\">Geosphere = <b>solid part</b> of Earth, <b>inner core to crust</b>: rocks, minerals, soil, mountains, volcanoes, landforms.</p>\n          <p>Foundation for land life; drives <b>plate tectonics, mountain formation, erosion</b>.</p>\n          <table class=\"tbl\"><thead><tr><th>Layer</th><th>What it is</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n            <tr><td>Inner core</td><td><b>Solid</b>, mainly <b>iron and nickel</b></td></tr>\n            <tr><td>Outer core</td><td><b>Liquid</b>; Earth's <b>magnetic field</b></td></tr>\n            <tr><td>Mantle</td><td><b>Semi-solid</b> rock; <b>convection currents</b> drive plate tectonics</td></tr>\n            <tr><td>Crust</td><td>Outermost; <b>continental and oceanic plates</b></td></tr>\n          </tbody></table>\n          <figure data-fig=\"m1-earth-layers\"></figure>\n        "},{"title":"Rocks, minerals, soil, landforms","html":"\n          <div class=\"kv\">\n            <div><span>Rocks</span>Natural solid aggregates of minerals or mineraloids. Three types: <b>igneous, sedimentary, metamorphic</b>.</div>\n            <div><span>Minerals</span>Natural, <b>inorganic</b> solids; <b>definite chemical composition</b>; <b>crystalline structure</b>.</div>\n            <div><span>Soil</span>Thin layer from <b>weathering of rocks</b> + <b>decay of organic matter</b>; has minerals, organic matter, water, air; supports plants, nutrient cycling.</div>\n            <div><span>Landforms</span>Mountains, valleys, plateaus, volcanoes, faults; from <b>tectonic processes</b> (earthquakes, eruptions, plate movement) + erosion and weathering.</div>\n          </div>\n          <h3>Geological processes</h3>\n          <ol>\n            <li><b>Plate tectonics</b>: earthquakes, volcanic eruptions, mountain building.</li>\n            <li><b>Weathering and erosion</b>: rocks broken down, carried by wind, water, ice.</li>\n            <li><b>Sedimentation</b>: sediments layer and harden into sedimentary rock.</li>\n          </ol>\n        "},{"title":"Importance and interactions","html":"\n          <h3>Why the geosphere matters</h3>\n          <ul>\n            <li><b>Supports ecosystems</b>: soil for plants, habitats.</li>\n            <li><b>Natural resources</b>: minerals, metals, fossil fuels.</li>\n            <li><b>Affects climate</b>: volcanic gases and ash cool or warm the atmosphere.</li>\n            <li><b>Physical geography</b>: elevation, terrain, where people settle.</li>\n          </ul>\n          <h3>Interactions with other spheres</h3>\n          <p>Interacts with atmosphere, hydrosphere, biosphere and <b>cryosphere</b>: a dynamic system.</p>\n          <table class=\"tbl\"><thead><tr><th>Sphere</th><th>Interaction</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n            <tr><td>Atmosphere</td><td>Volcanoes release <b>CO<sub>2</sub> and ash</b> (air quality, climate); wind and rain weather rocks.</td></tr>\n            <tr><td>Hydrosphere</td><td>Rivers <b>erode landforms</b>, carry sediment (valleys, coasts); groundwater affects erosion, mineral distribution.</td></tr>\n            <tr><td>Biosphere</td><td><b>Roots break rocks</b>, add organic matter; burrowing animals change soil structure.</td></tr>\n          </tbody></table>\n        "}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Composition and state of the inner core?","back":"<b>Solid</b>, mainly <b>iron and nickel</b>."},{"front":"Which layer produces Earth's magnetic field?","back":"The <b>liquid outer core</b>."},{"front":"What drives plate tectonics?","back":"<b>Convection currents</b> in the semi-solid mantle."}],"cardCount":8,"formulaCount":0},{"id":"m1-biosphere","title":"Biosphere","short":"Biosphere","blurb":"What the biosphere is, its living components and ecosystems, its functions, and how living things drive the carbon, nitrogen, oxygen and phosphorus cycles.","source":"Lecture W1S3, pages 12–14","tabs":[{"title":"Components and ecosystems","html":"\n          <p class=\"lead\">Biosphere = <b>global sum of all ecosystems</b>: all organisms and their environments, soil bacteria to the largest whales, across land, water and air.</p>\n          <figure data-fig=\"m1-biosphere-diagram\"></figure>\n          <h3>Living organisms</h3>\n          <div class=\"kv\">\n            <div><span>Plants</span><b>Primary producers</b>: photosynthesis; base of food chains</div>\n            <div><span>Animals</span><b>Consumers</b>: herbivores eat plants; carnivores, omnivores eat animals</div>\n            <div><span>Microorganisms</span>Bacteria, fungi, algae <b>decompose</b> dead matter, recycle nutrients, build soil fertility</div>\n          </div>\n          <figure data-fig=\"m1-food-chain-cycle\"></figure>\n          <h3>Ecosystems</h3>\n          <p>Community of organisms (<b>biotic</b>) interacting with non-living parts (<b>abiotic</b>: sunlight, soil, air, water). Examples:</p>\n          <div class=\"chips\"><span class=\"chip\">Forests</span><span class=\"chip\">Grasslands</span><span class=\"chip\">Deserts</span><span class=\"chip\">Freshwater (rivers, lakes)</span><span class=\"chip\">Marine (oceans, coral reefs)</span></div>\n        "},{"title":"Functions and cycles","html":"\n          <p class=\"lead\">Three functions: <b>supports life</b> (food, water, oxygen, habitat), <b>maintains biodiversity</b> (more species, more resilience), <b>regulates biogeochemical cycles</b> (carbon, nitrogen, oxygen, phosphorus).</p>\n          <figure data-fig=\"m1-biogeochemical-components\"></figure>\n          <table class=\"tbl\"><thead><tr><th>Cycle</th><th>How the biosphere runs it</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n            <tr><td>Carbon</td><td>Plants take in <b>CO<sub>2</sub></b> (photosynthesis), store carbon; animals eat plants; respiration, decomposition, burning fossil fuels release it.</td></tr>\n            <tr><td>Nitrogen</td><td>Soil/root bacteria convert <b>atmospheric nitrogen</b> into usable forms; moves up food chain; microbes return it by <b>denitrification</b>.</td></tr>\n            <tr><td>Oxygen</td><td>Plants make O<sub>2</sub>; animals use it, release CO<sub>2</sub> for plants: balanced oxygen–carbon loop.</td></tr>\n            <tr><td>Phosphorus</td><td>Taken from soil, used in <b>DNA and energy transfer</b>; decomposers return it to soil.</td></tr>\n          </tbody></table>\n        "}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Definition of the biosphere?","back":"The <b>global sum of all ecosystems</b> where life exists on Earth."},{"front":"Role of plants in the biosphere?","back":"<b>Primary producers</b>; base of food chains."},{"front":"Role of microorganisms in the biosphere?","back":"<b>Decompose</b> dead matter, recycle nutrients, add to soil fertility."}],"cardCount":6,"formulaCount":0}],"figures":[{"id":"m1-four-spheres","src":"pages/m1/evs-w1-02/p11.png","crop":[0.1,0.48,0.52,0.355],"caption":"Earth sits in the middle of the four spheres: atmosphere, hydrosphere, biosphere and lithosphere.","alt":"Circle divided into four photo segments labelled atmosphere, hydrosphere, biosphere and lithosphere around a picture of Earth."},{"id":"m1-air-composition","src":"pages/m1/evs-w1-02/p02.png","crop":[0.25,0.14,0.55,0.22],"caption":"Nitrogen is the big slice (78%), oxygen next (21%), and everything else is a sliver (1%).","alt":"Pie chart of air composition with nitrogen, oxygen and others."},{"id":"m1-atmosphere-layers-photo","src":"pages/m1/evs-w1-02/p02.png","crop":[0.13,0.53,0.79,0.35],"caption":"The five layers stacked above Earth, troposphere at the bottom and exosphere at the top.","alt":"Photo of Earth from space with bands labelled troposphere, stratosphere, mesosphere, thermosphere and exosphere."},{"id":"m1-stratosphere-profile","src":"pages/m1/evs-w1-02/p03.png","crop":[0.1,0.64,0.8,0.35],"caption":"Weather and clouds stay below the tropopause; the ozone layer, weather balloons and spy planes sit in the stratosphere; the red line shows temperature turning upward above the tropopause.","alt":"Altitude chart from the troposphere to the mesosphere with a temperature curve, aircraft, balloon, ozone layer and meteors."},{"id":"m1-ozone-uv","src":"pages/m1/evs-w1-02/p04.png","crop":[0.1,0.178,0.79,0.322],"caption":"Stratospheric ozone stops most incoming UV; surface-level ozone near cities is smog.","alt":"Diagram of UV-A, UV-B and UV-C rays reaching the stratospheric ozone layer above the troposphere."},{"id":"m1-mesosphere-diagram","src":"pages/m1/evs-w1-02/p05.png","crop":[0.1,0.133,0.8,0.347],"caption":"Troposphere, stratosphere with the ozone layer, and mesosphere, separated by the tropopause, stratopause and mesopause.","alt":"Curved bands showing troposphere, stratosphere including ozone layer and mesosphere, with UV and methane molecules."},{"id":"m1-atmosphere-poster","src":"pages/m1/evs-w1-02/p06.png","crop":[0.1,0.29,0.79,0.445],"caption":"Each layer with what you find there: planes in the troposphere, the ozone layer in the stratosphere, meteors in the mesosphere, satellites and auroras higher up.","alt":"Poster of the atmosphere layers with heights and objects in each layer."},{"id":"m1-water-cycle-spheres","src":"pages/m1/evs-w1-02/p08.png","crop":[0.17,0.18,0.62,0.32],"caption":"Evaporation, condensation, precipitation, runoff and collection make one loop.","alt":"Water cycle illustration with evaporation, condensation, precipitation, runoff and collection labels."},{"id":"m1-earth-layers","src":"pages/m1/evs-w1-02/p09.png","crop":[0.1,0.07,0.8,0.36],"caption":"From the outside in: crust, mantle, outer core and inner core.","alt":"Cut-away of Earth showing crust, mantle, outer core and inner core."},{"id":"m1-biosphere-diagram","src":"pages/m1/evs-w1-02/p12.png","crop":[0.12,0.34,0.53,0.37],"caption":"The biosphere overlaps the other three spheres: life is found in air, water and earth.","alt":"Illustration of the biosphere with labelled zones for atmosphere, hydrosphere and lithosphere."},{"id":"m1-food-chain-cycle","src":"pages/m1/evs-w1-02/p13.png","crop":[0.1,0.13,0.43,0.34],"caption":"Producer to primary, secondary, tertiary and final consumers, with fungi returning matter to the start.","alt":"Circular food chain from green plant through grasshopper, frog, snake and hawk, with fungi."},{"id":"m1-biogeochemical-components","src":"pages/m1/evs-w1-02/p14.png","crop":[0.1,0.178,0.55,0.268],"caption":"The biosphere sits on top of five cycles: carbon, phosphorus, nitrogen, water and oxygen.","alt":"Graphic of the biosphere above five columns labelled carbon, phosphorus, nitrogen, water and oxygen cycle."}]});
